{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790284104,"nonce":"Z3eMsEIq0Mffqn78zeiEsyiaMO4=","title":"wasm: Immix heap on wasm32, and fibers for compiled wasm bodies","message":"Pieces 2 and 3 of 407f14a, whose rooting work (piece 1) is done.\nParent epic: 6923f6e.\n\n1. Immix heap on wasm32: alloc_zeroed reservation aligned to the block\n   size, no hand-back, word stride 4, default size configurable\n   (today's nursery is 64 MiB per VM). Conservative scanning of\n   Rust-in-wasm frames is not available (locals are not in linear\n   memory), so the interpreter and helper roots stay precise on this\n   target.\n2. Fibers for compiled wasm bodies. Ash suspends compiled wasm with a\n   link-time unwind/rewind transform seeded from one host import\n   (env.ash_host_fiber_yield) and driven through three exported\n   globals; the host picks the mechanism (wasmtime async, JSPI, or the\n   transform). wren_lift emits its own wasm from MIR and already has a\n   state-machine transform for yielding functions, so apply that\n   transform to yielding MIR before wasm emission instead of rewriting\n   bytes after the fact. Route the actual suspension through a single\n   import so the browser host can use JSPI where available and the\n   worker/Promise bridge elsewhere. Functions that call yield then tier\n   up instead of staying interpreted.\n\nBuild the wasm artifacts in CI, run the wasm arm of the test corpus\nunder wasmtime (not a browser), keep the fibers-on arm last and diff\nits failure set against fibers-off so \"both green\" cannot hide a\ndivergence. bench.yml already runs wasm/checks/*.wren under Node\nagainst the fresh browser bundle; that is where a regression check for\neach piece goes.","files":null}]}